WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:08.080
[Standing on a busy street corner, a man lights up a cigarette.]

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:11.550
[Narrator:] The habit of smoking is just a
part of everyday life.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:14.900
[Head shots of people on the street puffing on cigarettes as they go about their lives.]

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:23.980
[A teenage boy is shown smoking, then two more men, then a grinning fellow with a cigarette tucked in his mouth.]

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:28.930
[Narrator:] These people really believe that
cigarettes mean something to them.

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:47.090
[Saxophone plays in the background as more smokers are shown.]

00:00:49.840 --> 00:01:00.780
[Music peters out as man smoking a cigarette makes a disgusted face.]

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.200
[Narrator:] The cigarette is a tube of paper filled with loosely packed, chopped-up tobacco

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:12.140
which burns slowly given the proper draft.
In many ways it can be compared with a chimney.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:16.430
When a chimney smokes badly, it means that the fuel in the fire is only being partially burnt.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:21.040
And all kinds of gases, dirt, and steam are
spreading into the air. 

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:25.680
No one would think of deliberately inhaling the thick smoke from this stack.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:30.120
The soot and gas would soon choke one to death.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:34.240
Yet a cigarette is also a chimney, and the
pleasure it seems to give people hides

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.810
the very real danger it holds for the lungs.

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:39.980
[A man smokes his cigarette with a slight
gasp for breath as he exhales the smoke.]

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:42.320
[Narrator:] The human lungs are a delicate
mechanism

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.080
and they can't be easily repaired if they get damaged.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:49.160
On the inside of the breathing tubes are very small hairs 

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.640
which are like eyelashes and are in constant movement.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:53.660
[A diagram of the lungs is shown with the
tiny hairs being blown back and forth.]

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:57.280
[Narrator:] On these, a layer of sticky substance called mucus falls.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:02.880
Which is constantly being moved up the breathing tube as if on a moving staircase.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.250
In the normal way, small bits of dirt which enter the throat

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:11.110
stick to this layer and are removed upwards. This is the lungs' defense against dirt.

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:14.890
[A larger diagram appears showing both lungs.]

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:18.340
[Narrator:] A smoker however, sucks in dust, smoke, and all kinds of dirt.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:22.959
[A man is shown smoking back-to-back cigarettes.]

00:02:22.959 --> 00:02:27.499
[Narrator:] Only a fraction of which is breathed out again.

00:02:27.499 --> 00:02:33.870
[The man exhales the smoke and makes another gasping sound.]

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:38.670
[Narrator:] The smoke inhaled temporarily
paralyzes the little hairs inside the throat.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:43.400
So that there is no moving escalator to protect the lung against dirt. 

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:47.160
Smoke also irritates and causes more liquid mucus to form.

00:02:48.639 --> 00:02:52.339
[Narrator:] Since it cannot be removed upwards, it falls back into the lungs. 

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:56.660
And if it stays there long, there's a danger of it plugging up some of the smaller tubes.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:00.740
Infection grows in these warm places,

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:04.420
and a cough on top of trapped air works like bursting a paper bag.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:06.040
[Man coughs]

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:11.700
[Narrator:] If much of this goes on, the damage done becomes really dangerous. 

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:14.200
The victim becomes permanently breathless.

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:15.920
And then, ill.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.740
[The lungs are shown with widespread damage due to cigarette smoke.]

00:03:17.749 --> 00:03:23.139
[Narrator:] A healthy lung looks like this.
A more or less regular fine mesh of tissues.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:28.220
But much of the delicate structure of this
lung has been destroyed by smoking.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:29.419
[A damaged lung is shown in fine detail.]

00:03:29.419 --> 00:03:32.969
[Narrator:] A man with a lung like this is
permanently breathless.

00:03:32.969 --> 00:03:36.769
And often too ill to get about at all.

00:03:36.769 --> 00:03:40.840
[An elderly man is lying in bed wheezing for breath.]

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:44.139
[Narrator:] This man use to be a heavy smoker. Now he's a helpless invalid.

00:03:44.139 --> 00:03:46.699
[Man continues to gasp for air.]

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:53.880
[Doctor:] I just want you to step up and down on these steps Mr. Major, as I tell you.

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:58.340
Right. Come up, up there. Why can't you. That's right. Down the other side. Turn around.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:00.660
[Mr. Major walks up a step and then down the other side where he turns around to do it all over again.]

00:04:00.969 --> 00:04:06.109
[Narrator:] Two years ago this man could swim with his teenage son. But now he can't.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:09.020
[Doctor:] Now would you like to sit down now?

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:10.300
[Mr. Major:] Sure.

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:13.779
[Doctor:] Is that about as much as you can
do?

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:16.500
[Mr. Major:] [Gasping for breath] That's all,
please, I can't do anymore.

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:19.940
[Narrator:] No one has to tell his son not
to smoke.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:29.760
[A hand is shown lighting cigarettes attached to individual containers lined up in a row.]

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:37.380
[Narrator:] This is a smoking machine which was specially invented to imitate the way a person smokes.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:40.630
And to collect scientifically the tobacco
smoke which goes into the body.

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:42.390
[Cigarettes burn into the smoking machine;
an inhaling sound is heard.]

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:48.680
[Narrator:] The throat of the mechanical smoker is a glass tube. And the lungs, a glass bottle.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:53.430
And it smokes like six people in a row.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:58.900
[Inhaling and exhaling sounds.]

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:00.760
Every puff is counted.

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:07.480
[Inhaling and exhaling sounds.]

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:16.080
[Narrator:] After being given only a packet
of only ten each, their throats are stained with tar.

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:23.710
[The doctor examines the tar buildup.]

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:28.560
[Narrator:] Even this small amount can be
taken away and analyzed. 

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:30.440
And if the mechanical smoker goes on long enough,

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:33.030
you can soon fill jars with cigarette tar.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:35.740
[A jar full of hot tar is shown beside an
ashtray filled with cigarette butts.]

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:42.140
[Narrator:] A week, a month, a year, five
years of smoking twenty a day would fill these jars.

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:47.550
This enormous beaker would be filled after
twenty a day for twenty years.

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:49.600
Of course not all this stays in the body.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:52.140
But part of it does.

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:57.080
Scientists examined this tar and found many complicated chemicals in it.

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:00.700
Though smoking is not the only cause of lung
cancer, it is the major one.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:06.460
And at least sixteen of these
chemicals are already known to produce cancer.

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:10.440
Research is still going on to find out how
these work in smokers. 

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:14.000
It's taking a long time because one can't experiment on live people.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:15.960
[A teenage boy stands shirtless as a doctor prepares to examine him.]

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:20.020
[Narrator:] As a result of what scientists
have discovered and doctors know,

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:24.440
it's difficult now to find a chest doctor in this country who is still smoking cigarettes.

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:26.320
And here's another reason.

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:32.640
Imagine these two orange blocks as representing in tens of millions all the cigarettes smoked in 1910.

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:36.540
Here are the amounts which have been smoked since then up to 1960.

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:41.580
[Stacks of orange blocks appear behind a year
to represent the number of cigarettes smoked.]

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:45.380
[Narrator:] Compare this to the numbers of
deaths from lung cancer during the same years.

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:51.880
They're in thousands but still going up and
closely following the rising cigarette sales.

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:55.730
Though not everyone who smokes gets lung cancer,
no one knows who will.

00:06:55.730 --> 00:06:57.790
[Black blocks are stacked in front of
the orange blocks to represent the number of lung cancer-related death in those years.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:03.880
[Narrator:] Lung cancer is an increasing menace. Why? Because people are encouraging it by smoking.

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:07.760
[Doctor Two:] There's no mistake about this
[?]. Cigarettes can kill,

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:10.220
and if they don't kill they can rot the lungs.

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:14.920
In this bottle is the lung of a man who was
a moderate smoker

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:17.600
 'til this great cancer grew in his lung and killed him.

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:18.960
[The doctor points to a lung with a large tumor in a specimen jar.]

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:21.890
[Doctor Two:] If this man had known what cigarettes were going to do to his lungs, 

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:25.760
he wouldn't have smoked. You do know.

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:31.240
[Narrator:] When one is young and feeling
fit one thinks the body can look after itself.

00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:34.250
Even when one realizes that smoking can lead to chronic bronchitis

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:39.070
or heart disease as well as lung cancer, it's
a habit which is very difficult to give up.

00:07:39.070 --> 00:07:40.960
[A teenage girl is shown smoking, looking at herself in a mirror.]

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:44.290
[Narrator:] These boys and girls may think
it's clever or grown-up to be seen with a cigarette,

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:49.640
but they don't realize that for every day
they smoke they could actually be shortening their own lives.

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:58.740
[Young people and children are seen casually smoking.]

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:02.100
[Narrator:] Smoking in public may seem daring, but it's hardly glamorous.

00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:06.430
[A group of women sit in their train car smoking and putting on make-up.]

00:08:06.430 --> 00:08:11.100
[Narrator:] This side of smoking is never
shown in advertising.

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:21.620
[A man is shown smoking, but grimacing and looking as if it is distasteful.]

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:30.080
[A man leans over an object he appears to
be repairing, a cigarette dangling from his mouth.]

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:37.520
[A man puts out his cigarette on a
plate with the remains of his breakfast eggs.]

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:43.820
[A cigarette butt is left inside someone's
teacup.]

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:50.320
[Doctor Two:] If you do smoke, well, stop
it. But it's much easier not to start then to stop.

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:55.140
Don't think you can't stop. I used to say
that, but then I saw the damage that cigarettes

00:08:55.150 --> 00:08:59.200
were doing to my patients...and I stopped.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:03.910
If you don't smoke you will save hundreds
of wasted pounds and you'll stay fit.

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:05.390
[Doctor listens to the same teenage boy's chest again.]

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:07.160
[Doctor:] Now breathe deeply.

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:16.240
[The boy is heard breathing in and out, deeply.]

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:21.540
[Narrator:] People with healthy lungs can
really enjoy life.

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:28.540
[A man with healthy lungs goes swimming deep
underwater.]

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:35.640
[A man plays a saxophone beautifully.]

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:42.940
[Two men play-fight underwater.]

00:09:42.980 --> 00:09:46.920
[Saxophonist plays.]

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:50.060
[Narrator:] But for people who are smokers,
it's a different story.

00:09:50.140 --> 00:09:54.920
[A man is shown rolling a cigarette, and Mr. Majors is seen again, grimacing.]

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:57.360
[Damaged lung tissue is displayed.]

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:01.800
[The man lying in a hospital bed gasping for air is shown.]

00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:07.480
[A man coughs.]

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:09.600
[The orange and black blocks representing smokers and cancer deaths flash by.]

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:13.600
[A specimen jar containing portions of  damaged lung is shown.]

00:10:14.140 --> 00:10:19.680
[The ashtray and jar of tar is shown filled
to the top.]

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:30.660
[Gasping, labored breathing sounds.]

